-- title:
--   The TAP free energy (2.1), the map $\widehat y$, the partition function $Z_{\beta A,y}$, and the processes SL-TAP and PHD-TAP driven by stochastic localization
-- statement:
--   **TAP free energy.** `hEnt` is $h(x)=-\tfrac{1+x}{2}\log\tfrac{1+x}{2}-\tfrac{1-x}{2}\log\tfrac{1-x}{2}$ and `FTAP β A m y` is
--   $$\mathcal F_{\mathrm{TAP}}(m,y)=-\frac\beta2\langle m,Am\rangle-\langle y,m\rangle-\sum_{i=1}^nh(m_i)-\frac14n\beta^2\Big(1-\frac{\|m\|_2^2}{n}\Big)^2.$$
--   `yhat β A m` is $\widehat y(m)$, the unique $y$ with $\nabla_m\mathcal F_{\mathrm{TAP}}(m,y)=0$:
--   $$\widehat y(m)=-\beta Am+\tfrac12\log\frac{1+m}{1-m}+\beta^2\Big(1-\frac{\|m\|_2^2}{n}\Big)m.$$
--   `partFn β A y` is $Z_{\beta A,y}=\sum_{\sigma\in\{\pm1\}^n}\exp\big(\tfrac\beta2\langle\sigma,A\sigma\rangle+\langle y,\sigma\rangle\big)$.
--
--   **Processes driven by stochastic localization.** Let $y_t$ be a tilt process with magnetization $m_t$, for a (possibly random) matrix $A$. `innov y mag` is the Brownian motion $B_t=y_t-\int_0^tm_s\,ds$ of (SL), $dy_t=m_t\,dt+dB_t$, and `tiltPast P A y` is the completed filtration generated by $A$ and $(y_s)_{s\le t}$.
--
--   - `IsSLTAP P β c A y mag M`: $M=m^y$ is the SL-TAP process, $dm^y_t=\widehat Q(m^y_t)\big((m_t-f(m^y_t))\,dt+dB_t\big)$, $m^y_0=0$, stopped at its first exit from $\mathcal S_{\beta,A}(c)$.
--   - `IsPHDTAP P β c A y mag Mh Yh`: $(\widehat m,\widehat y)$ is the PHD-TAP pair driven by the same $B$: $d\widehat m_t=\widehat Q(\widehat m_t)\,dB_t$, $d\widehat y_t=f(\widehat m_t)\,dt+dB_t$, $\widehat m_0=\widehat y_0=0$, both stopped at the first exit of $\widehat m$ from $\mathcal S_{\beta,A}(c)$.
--   - `SLQuenched β A P`: stochastic localization for one fixed matrix $A$: $\sigma\sim\mu_{\beta A}$ and an independent standard Brownian motion $B$, with $y_t=t\sigma+B_t$ (`y`) and $m_t=\langle\sigma\rangle_{y_t}$ (`mag`).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Display (2.3) of the paper, meant to define $\widehat y$, repeats the right-hand side of (2.1); `yhat` is the solution of the displayed equation (TAP), which is what the text defines. The stopped Itô processes are those of the dynamics definitions, built on the published `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral`.
-- source:
--   H. Lee, J. S. Sandhu, J. Shi, Potential Hessian Ascent IV: Sampling the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model at β < 1, https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30590, Definition 2.1 and (2.1) p. 4; (TAP), (SL), (PHD-TAP) p. 7; (SL-TAP) p. 8; proof of Lemma 3.3 p. 11; proof of Lemma 3.6 p. 13

